<CaseStudy slug="ai-context-layer">
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 pt-2 sm:px-10">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        New Relic is building its first AI-native product, and personalization
        is central to it: the layer that shapes how the agent adapts to each
        user, team, and organization. I shape the UX vision and strategy for that
        layer. That means defining how personalization should work, and
        partnering with product on the roadmap that prioritizes it. I ground the
        direction in research and analytics, so it drives the outcomes that
        matter, like deeper engagement and faster time-to-resolution. The goal is AI capabilities
        that feel seamless, natural, and cohesive across every surface of the
        product.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </section>

  <CaseSection
    title="Defining what personalization should mean for an observability agent."
  >
    <Prose>
      <p>
        "We want personalization" is easy to say and hard to specify. The open
        question wasn't whether to personalize — it was what the layers should
        be, how they'd compose across an organization, how team-based operations
        would interact with the model, and what "working" would even look like. I
        led the design thinking that turned that ambition into a concrete,
        buildable, measurable direction.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection title="Mapping the layered context model to observability.">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        The taxonomy — identity, operational graph, indexed knowledge, team
        defaults, declared rules, learned procedural and semantic memory,
        user-authored templates — draws directly on how industry AI products
        think about context. My contribution was working through which layers
        matter most for observability, how they'd interact with things specific
        to this domain (the entity graph, the on-call schedule, alert policies,
        existing runbooks), and where the observability-specific decisions
        actually live. If you can show the matrix visually, do — it's the
        clearest artifact of the applied thinking.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection
    title="Personalizing at the team and org level, not just the user."
  >
    <Prose>
      <p>
        New Relic's AI experience personalizes at the user, team, and
        organization levels — an architecture that differs from most consumer AI.
        For a team-based operations tool, the team layer is load-bearing: it's
        where shared runbooks, ownership, and on-call defaults live. Getting that
        layer right is what makes personalization feel cohesive across a whole
        organization, not just tuned to one person.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection title="Framing success in a form the team could measure.">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        Adapted the learning-curve idea — same user, same task, day 60 quality
        vs day 1 — as a way to make "personalization is working" concrete for PM
        and engineering. Not a novel evaluation concept in AI, but naming it
        clearly gave the team a shared target and moved the conversation from
        "add personalization" to "here's what we'd need to see to believe it's
        helping."
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection title="What I delivered">
    <Prose>
      <h3>Competitive analysis of AI-native products</h3>
      <p>
        A survey of how comparable AI products handle personalization, memory,
        and user context — spanning consumer AI assistants, coding agents, and
        enterprise AI copilots. I distilled the industry patterns and gaps into a
        reference the team could point to when making product decisions, and used
        it to surface which patterns translate cleanly to observability and which
        don't.
      </p>

      <h3>Context-layer taxonomy matrix</h3>
      <p>
        A layered model mapping every source of context the agent might draw on —
        identity, operational graph, indexed knowledge, team defaults, declared
        rules, learned memory, user-authored templates — against properties like
        authoring, update cadence, scope, decay risk, and user visibility.{" "}
        <strong>Purpose:</strong> give the team a shared vocabulary for reasoning
        about which layers to build first and how they should compose.
      </p>

      <h3>Team-level rules and behaviors matrix</h3>
      <p>
        A companion matrix concretely illustrating what team-level
        personalization looks like in an observability context — vocabulary,
        signal preferences, response format, workflow defaults, knowledge
        sources, boundaries, routing. <strong>Purpose:</strong> make the abstract
        "team layer" tangible enough to design and build against.
      </p>

      <h3>Signals matrix for measuring personalization effectiveness</h3>
      <p>
        A mapping of candidate evaluation signals — friction signals, outcome
        alignment (hypothesis → RCA), trust signals (unmodified-use,
        action-confirmation), hygiene metrics — to the layers of the
        personalization stack they diagnose. <strong>Purpose:</strong> make "is
        personalization working?" answerable in a form PM and engineering could
        operationalize.
      </p>

      <h3>Design framework for the context layer</h3>
      <p>
        A set of applied design decisions connecting the abstract context model
        to concrete product surfaces: the inheritance model (org → team → user),
        citation and provenance in agent responses, the inspection-and-correction
        surface ("what does the agent know about me?"), and cold-start behavior
        for new users and new teams. <strong>Purpose:</strong> bridge the
        systems-level thinking to decisions the product team could actually build
        against.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </CaseSection>
</CaseStudy>
